## Why This Is Asked

Real-time network paths require precise tail latency insight without burdening performance. eBPF offers low-overhead, per-event visibility ideal for diagnosing bursts in UDP data paths.

## Key Concepts

- eBPF tracing (bpftrace/bpfcc) for live latency data
- Tracepoints vs kprobes for kernel events
- Per-CPU histograms and BPF maps for aggregation
- Data-path isolation with tc and minimal instrumentation
- Safe rollback/guardrails if overhead grows

## Code Example

```javascript
function percentile(arr, p){
  if (!arr.length) return 0;
  const sorted = [...arr].sort((a,b)=>a-b);
  const idx = Math.ceil((sorted.length-1)*p/100);
  return sorted[idx];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this plan if TLS/DTLS terminates before the RX path?
- What thresholds and sampling rates would you choose for a fleet of hosts, and how would you automate rollback across them?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world WAL tail latency can throttle throughput in bursts; this question probes capability to diagnose IO path bottlenecks and apply production-grade mitigations that don’t compromise data integrity.

## Key Concepts
- IO scheduling and blk-mq
- Per-disk and per-tenant I/O control (cgroups v2)
- CPU/IRQ affinity and NUMA awareness
- WAL isolation strategies and PostgreSQL tuning
- Rigorous load-testing and safe rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js: simple iostat collector for WAL latency analysis
const {spawn} = require('child_process');
const log = require('fs').createWriteStream('/var/log/iostat_wal.log', {flags:'a'});
const proc = spawn('iostat', ['-x','1']);
proc.stdout.on('data', data => log.write(data));
proc.stderr.on('data', d => process.stderr.write(d));
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify data durability isn’t compromised during rollback?
- Which metrics/logs would you baseline before and after changes, and how would you automate rollback if thresholds are not met?
